            <div xml:id="d152">

                <pb facs="0406" n="262" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg262"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/148: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-07-31T11:00:00+01:00">July 31, 1929—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 31—9:40 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>139. Embassy’s telegram 137, July 30, 11 a.m.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d152-f1"/> By
                    an official communiqué published in the newspapers of this morning, local Soviet
                    Embassy reiterates earlier denials of direct negotiations with the Chinese
                    Minister here and denies in particular latest reports stated to come from
                    Washington that the Soviet Embassy has “sounded” Chinese Legation in Berlin in
                    connection with the railway conflict.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
